{
  "id": "complement-of-a-number",
  "questionId": "complement-of-a-number",
  "slug": "complement-of-a-number",
  "title": "Complement of a Number",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "flipping every bit of n is the same as XOR-ing n with a value that has a 1 in every position n occupies - what mask do you need?",
    "you need a mask of all 1s that's exactly as wide as n's binary representation, not wider (extra leading 1s would flip bits n doesn't have)",
    "find the smallest power of two greater than n (call it p) - then p - 1 is exactly a same-width all-ones mask, e.g. n=5 needs width 3, p=8, mask=7 (111)",
    "the answer is n XOR mask, where mask is built by starting at 1 and left-shifting until it exceeds n, then subtracting 1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a fixed 32-bit mask (like flipping all bits of a standard integer) instead of a mask sized to n's own bit width, which gives a huge wrong answer",
    "off-by-one when finding the smallest power of two greater than n (using >= vs > incorrectly)",
    "forgetting the special case n = 0 has no bits to flip, if 0 is allowed by the constraints"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a non-negative integer `n`.\n\n**Return the complement of `n`, formed by flipping every bit within `n`'s own minimal binary representation, as an integer.**\n\nThe flip only applies to the bits `n` actually needs, with no leading zero padding beyond that width.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 1 (complement is 0)",
      "n whose binary form is all 1s (e.g. 7 = 111), complement is 0",
      "n that is a power of two (e.g. 8 = 1000), complement is all 1s below that bit"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
